{
 "id": "TD-10",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-td-10-e2740fd1cd",
 "endReason": "stuck",
 "customerTurns": 6,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "dryer wont stop"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is main pcb. Open the door, make sure no clothing is caught in it, then shut it firmly until it clicks and press start. Does it still show the door as open?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its not showing door open, it says End. the drum just turns every few minutes after it finishes"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "I can't confirm what END means on your model, so I'll go by what it is doing. From what you have described, the most likely cause is door interlock / switch (electrical start path). With the dryer unplugged, look at the catch on the door and the slot it clicks into: fluff or a bit of fabric can stop it engaging, and check the door lines up when you close it. Is the catch broken, was something stopping it (now cleared), or does it all look fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I havent looked yet. The door seems to shut fine and it stops when I open it. Dries the clothes ok though"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Does a timed programme dry fine while only the AutoDry/sensor programme misbehaves, and does it end too early or never finish?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't tried a timed one. It dries fine and doesn't end early, it's just the turning after it says End"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is check the programme and load (try a timed programme; sensor needs a reasonable load). Behind the flap at the bottom front there's a condenser unit (or a second filter on heat-pump models). Take it out and rinse the fluff off under the tap, then let it drip-dry and refit it. Was it clogged with fluff (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the condenser and water tank are\" — On a condenser dryer the water container pulls out at the top and the condenser/heat exchanger sits behind a flap at the bottom front — slide it out and rinse it. Condenser/heat-pump dryers only; layout may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't taken it out yet. Will that stop the turning after it says End?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, the tumble dryer should stop turning once the cycle completes and displays \"End\". If it continues to run after this message appears, there is likely a fault with the control board or timer. From what you have described, the most likely cause is main pcb. Slide the water container out, empty it, check it isn't cracked and that the lid / float is clean, then push it firmly all the way home until it clicks. Was it full or not pushed fully in (and is it sorted), is it cracked, or was it already empty, seated and fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't looked at the water container yet. Is that going to stop the turning too? Its only 3 months old and I'm worried its wasting electricity"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, if the tumble dryer is displaying \"END,\" it has completed its cycle and should stop tumbling. If the drum continues to turn after this message appears, there may be a fault with the control system or timer. From what you have described, the most likely cause is check the programme and load (try a timed programme; sensor needs a reasonable load). Is the container actually staying empty with water pooling elsewhere, and is the condenser/sump clear of fluff?"
  }
 ],
